egin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186132361 :answer-field/correct "answer-603e20fb-5fe2-5ee6-a1ec-6a40034bec5f"] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices "answer-bb2cea96-c7d9-5a98-9f05-cc71f90c9189"] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices "answer-b8e540cd-3c04-55e6-a37b-b458bf640947"] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices "answer-a6fc93be-036b-5b3e-bc66-6cf227beda4f"] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices "answer-603e20fb-5fe2-5ee6-a1ec-6a40034bec5f"] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices "answer-ff3e0dd1-2a05-50c1-b114-ba517fd9603a"] ["question-bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279" :question/id #uuid "bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279"] ["question-bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279" :question/math-academy-id "q-139932"] ["question-bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$7$** | **$5$** | **$3$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] ["question-bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$7$** | **$5$** | **$3$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] ["question-bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/id #uuid "08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549"] ["answer-6d35d5af-1a30-585e-b04a-4b9d43448d86" :answer/id #uuid "6d35d5af-1a30-585e-b04a-4b9d43448d86"] ["answer-6d35d5af-1a30-585e-b04a-4b9d43448d86" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6d35d5af-1a30-585e-b04a-4b9d43448d86" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-79f6ba33-312c-5627-95ab-ce2b3d55ffe0" :answer/id #uuid "79f6ba33-312c-5627-95ab-ce2b3d55ffe0"] ["answer-79f6ba33-312c-5627-95ab-ce2b3d55ffe0" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-79f6ba33-312c-5627-95ab-ce2b3d55ffe0" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-bf8c68d4-b0dc-5d0b-a0df-3653df84c907" :answer/id #uuid "bf8c68d4-b0dc-5d0b-a0df-3653df84c907"] ["answer-bf8c68d4-b0dc-5d0b-a0df-3653df84c907" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-bf8c68d4-b0dc-5d0b-a0df-3653df84c907" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-71f24dc2-0e5f-5cd3-ba5c-65c375e4e4a1" :answer/id #uuid "71f24dc2-0e5f-5cd3-ba5c-65c375e4e4a1"] ["answer-71f24dc2-0e5f-5cd3-ba5c-65c375e4e4a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-71f24dc2-0e5f-5cd3-ba5c-65c375e4e4a1" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-315beb70-a5ce-5c37-a5c9-c0eca737c77e" :answer/id #uuid "315beb70-a5ce-5c37-a5c9-c0eca737c77e"] ["answer-315beb70-a5ce-5c37-a5c9-c0eca737c77e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-315beb70-a5ce-5c37-a5c9-c0eca737c77e" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/correct "answer-71f24dc2-0e5f-5cd3-ba5c-65c375e4e4a1"] ["answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/choices "answer-6d35d5af-1a30-585e-b04a-4b9d43448d86"] ["answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/choices "answer-79f6ba33-312c-5627-95ab-ce2b3d55ffe0"] ["answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/choices "answer-bf8c68d4-b0dc-5d0b-a0df-3653df84c907"] ["answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/choices "answer-71f24dc2-0e5f-5cd3-ba5c-65c375e4e4a1"] ["answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/choices "answer-315beb70-a5ce-5c37-a5c9-c0eca737c77e"] ["question-bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549"] [17592186133809 :knowledge-point/questions "question-bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279"] [17592186135064 :tutorial/content "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**."] [17592186135145 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution."] [17592186134031 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186134387 :question/problem "| $x_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y = β_{1}x_{1} + β_{2}x_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{1} \\\\ β_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y$,\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices 17592186132362] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices 17592186132363] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices 17592186132364] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices 17592186132365] [17592186132361 :answer-field/choices 17592186132366] [17592186135064 :tutorial/content "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2} &=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2} &=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2} &=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2} &=24\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**."] [17592186135145 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution."]]}]